<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Free County of Burgundy</h1><p class="st-meta">982 CE – 1678 CE (approx.) · capital Dole</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Franche-Comté, the imperial county of Burgundy across the Saône, rich in salt and fiercely loyal to its Habsburg rulers until Louis XIV conquered it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Otto-William, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berengar-ii-of-italy">Berengar of Italy</a>, gathered the counties east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a> in the 980s and founded the comital house. The county lay in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-arles">Kingdom of Arles</a> and so in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, unlike the neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy</a>, which was a French fief.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Salt springs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salins-les-bains">Salins</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lons-le-saunier">Lons-le-Saunier</a> provided the county's wealth, and a parliament sat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dole">Dole</a>. Countess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beatrice-i-countess-of-burgundy">Beatrice</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> in 1156, tying the county to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a>. Through marriage it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a> in 1384 and joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/burgundian-state">Burgundian State</a>, then went with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-of-burgundy">Mary of Burgundy</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and the Spanish crown.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> invaded in 1668 and again in 1674. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-nijmegen">Treaty of Nijmegen</a> in 1678 gave the county to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a>, which moved its capital to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/imperial-city-of-besancon">Besançon</a>. Its lasting name, Franche-Comté, recalls its separate past.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-county-of-burgundy.jpg" alt="Free County of Burgundy" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Damouns · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Franche-Comt%C3%A9.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>982 CE – 1678 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dole</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Otto-William of Burgundy, Beatrice I of Burgundy, Otto IV of Burgundy</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Franche-Comté, County of Burgundy</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1330&amp;focus=free-county-of-burgundy" aria-label="Free County of Burgundy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4809 525 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free County of Burgundy lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5129 781L5111 886M5129 781L5229 694M5129 781L5118 774M5129 781L4910 989M5129 781L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5111 886h0M5229 694h0M5118 774h0M4910 989h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5129 781h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5129 781h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Dole. <a href="/atlas?year=1330&amp;focus=free-county-of-burgundy">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

